### Justification
The claim is misleading through cherry-picking: while Scientific American did publish Joel Moskowitz's October 2019 opinion piece titled ["We Have No Reason to Believe 5G Is Safe"](https://www.saferemr.com/2020/02/will-scientific-american-clear-up.html), the magazine simultaneously published opposing opinion pieces—Kenneth Foster's ["5G Is Coming: How Worried Should We Be about the Health Risks?"](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/scientific-american-created-confusion-5gs-safety-clear-joel-moskowitz) and David Robert Grimes' ["Don't Fall Prey to Scaremongering about 5G"](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/scientific-american-created-confusion-5gs-safety-clear-joel-moskowitz)—and never issued an official editorial warning against 5G, instead adopting a stance of balance and acknowledging scientific uncertainty. The [naturalnews.com article](https://www.naturalnews.com/2020-10-20-scientific-american-issues-warnings-dangers-of-5g.html) by Cassie B, published on a source rated as a [questionable and discredited outlet](https://mediabiasfactcheck.com/natural-news/) that "routinely publishes false information," misrepresented a single author's opinion as the magazine's institutional position, a framing echoed but not corrected by the [ACSH analysis](https://www.acsh.org/news/2019/10/21/scientific-american-says-5g-dangerous-vegetables-are-junk-food-14351). The claim is therefore technically true on a narrow reading but deeply misleading in its implication, omitting the contradictory context that fundamentally changes the impression conveyed.